Now I think _you_ are right, and _me_ wrong, which
brings me back to a problem with both C and how
NetHack is written using it.

C tends to lead programmers to express too many
checks in the negative and then to short circuiting
calculations with them, rather than expressing
checks in the affirmative and then doing the actions
those checks protect.

[One of the reasons for this, in turn, is that
"standard" C coding style doesn't stack braces,
but puts the opening brace at the end of the opening
line making deep nesting very hard to understand,
and so shortcuts are used to avoid deep nesting.]

Written as I prefer to see structured code written,
so the logic and nesting levels of boolean operators
are easily identified, the affirmative version of
just the above code (the original function is more
complex than this) would read:

   if
   (
     (
       (
         depth( &u.uz )
         >
         0
       )
       &&
       (
         (
           rn2( 1 + ( ( depth( &u.uz ) / 4 ) ) )
         )
         >
         0
       )
     )
   )
   {
     return /* some ok_to_drop_bones result */;
   }

Among other things, that style would cut down on the
size and complexity of the huge meandering functions
in current NetHack.

